  "query_preview": "../Wednesday through Saturday/ \nThe active pattern continues through midweek, as a weak plume of \nmoisture pushes into the NE Gulf coast Tuesday before beginning to\nfall apart as it moves eastward Wednesday. High pressure \ncontinues to build over BC just to the east of the panhandle, and \na ridge building over the Gulf beginning to move over the \npanhandle by Wednesday will work to keep much of the area drier. \nThough some parts of the northern panhandle will see some of the \nremnant shortwave move through, keeping rain (and snow at the \nupper elevations of the northern highways) chances into Wednesday \nmorning and afternoon as this plume pushes east. But overall the \nsouthern half of the panhandle, generally from Frederick Sound \nsouthward, will see drier and clearer conditions Wednesday due to \nthe high pressure building over the area. \n\nThe next system moving into the Gulf Thursday into Friday remains\na bit uncertain still in terms of timing and what areas will see \nprecipitation, largely dependent on if ridging aloft is strong \nenough to block the upper level low from moving eastward, if the \nhigh pressure will keep the panhandle clear and the front more to \nthe west. Confidence has increased on this system pushing into the\nnorthern coastline Thursday, largely directed into areas to the \nwest of Yakutat. Chances remain of it pushing eastward during the \nday Thursday, with the highest probabilities amongst the panhandle\nbeing for Yakutat and the NE Gulf coastline, while only slight \nchances of pushing into more of the panhandle by Friday. This has \ncome into agreement across the models that it will be kept more \noffshore, though the potential for impacting the outer coast \nremain a possibility depending on the setup and strength of the \nhigh pressure. Overall precipitation chances diminish in the late \nweek into the weekend as guidance really begins to split, with a \ncolder and drier pattern with the high over BC looking to be the \nmost likely scenario at this time.",
